    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Where Is It?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Go to the Window.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reading: reading_1: Participate in choral reading sessions, supported by the teacher.
reading_2: Match spoken words to their written form as they are heard and supported with pictures.
phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reading: Can participate in choral reading with teacher guidance, recognizing familiar location words (e.g., 'under,' 'in,' 'on') in stories.
Can match objects or characters in picture books by pointing when asked questions about location (e.g., 'Where is the cat?').
Can recognize the names of letters (e.g., K, L, M, N, O) during choral reading activities (e.g., pointing to 'O' in 'orange' and saying the name of the letter O during choral reading activities).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276; width: 45%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276; width: 45%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" style TPR activity focusing on classroom objects and simple prepositions. Teacher says, "Simon says, 'point to the book!'" or "Simon says, 'put your hand on the desk!'" Students perform the actions. The teacher includes verbs like 'point,' 'hold,' 'pick,' 'put,' and nouns like 'chair,' 'desk,' 'book,' 'bag,' 'pencil.' This activates prior knowledge of vocabulary and prepositions from Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a large picture flashcard of a cat *under* a table. Teacher says clearly, "The cat is under the table." The teacher points to the cat, then makes an "under" gesture. Then, the teacher holds up a flashcard with the written word "under" and slowly traces the letters, saying "U-N-D-E-R, under." The teacher also models the /w/ sound in "where" by emphasizing mouth shape and repeating "Where? Where?" and the /b/ sound in "book" by showing lip closure and repeating "Book. Book."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a cat *on* the table) "Is the cat *under* the table?" (Students shake head for No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a book) "Is this a book or a pencil?" (Students say "Book!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Where Is It?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large "Big Book" picture story titled "Where Is My Pencil?" The story features a child looking for a pencil (using 'pencil,' 'book,' 'desk,' 'chair,' 'bag,' 'box') and finding it in different locations (e.g., "The pencil is on the book.", "The pencil is under the chair.", "The pencil is in the bag."). Each page has a large, clear illustration and one simple sentence. The text font is large and easy to read for young learners.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using location words: Respond with basic words to location-based questions, using prepositions (e.g., 'The book is on the desk.'). Following location commands: Move to designated areas in response to instructions (e.g., 'Go to the window.'). Pragmatic competence is key in understanding the context of location.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to each word as they read, ensuring students follow along. Students focus on the pictures to understand the main idea of where the pencil is going.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Visual):</b> After the first read-through, the teacher reads a sentence, e.g., "The pencil is under the chair." Students point to the corresponding picture in the book or on a separate set of flashcards.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Yes/No Questions with Visuals:</b> Teacher reads a sentence, e.g., "The pencil is on the desk." Then asks, "Is the pencil under the desk?" Students respond with a visual cue (thumbs up for yes, thumbs down for no) while looking at the picture in the book.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Participate in choral reading sessions, supported by the teacher. Match spoken words to their written form. Recognize familiar letters. Grammar: Prepositions of place (in, on, under, next to, behind, in front of). Vocabulary: chair, desk, book, bag, box, pencil. Pronunciation: /w/ in 'where', /b/ in 'book'. Phonemic Awareness: K, L, M, N, O.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Choral Reading and Word Tracing (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher displays the "Big Book" again. The teacher reads each sentence slowly, pointing to each word. Students chorally repeat the sentence, pointing to the words in the book along with the teacher. The teacher specifically highlights words like "Where" and "book" to practice /w/ and /b/ sounds. Students are also encouraged to air-trace the words as they read them aloud.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Picture-to-Word Matching Cards (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher distributes small cards with pictures (e.g., a chair, a desk, a book, a pencil, a bag) and separate cards with the corresponding written words. Students are instructed to match each picture card to its correct word card. The teacher circulates, providing support and checking matches. Emphasis is placed on 'reading' the word by matching its visual form to the known picture.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Letter Hunt and Recognition (Guided Practice):</b> The teacher points to various words in the "Big Book" (e.g., 'book,' 'pencil,' 'on'). The teacher asks students to find and point to specific letters that were introduced in the learning outcome for phonemic awareness (K, L, M, N, O) within these words. For instance, "Can you find the letter 'O' in 'on'?" or "Look for the letter 'L' in 'pencil'." Students point to the letter, and the teacher encourages them to say the name of the letter.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Where Is It?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Where Is It?" Picture Story Reading. Students work in pairs or small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a simplified, short version of the "Where Is My Pencil?" story on a large sheet of paper or a small booklet. Each page has one large picture and one short sentence (e.g., "The book is on the desk.", "The bag is under the chair.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student "reads" a page by pointing to the words and the picture. The others listen and follow along, pointing to the corresponding objects in their miniature story.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, another student asks a simple "Where is...?" question related to their page, like "Where is the book?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The first student, or another group member, responds by pointing to the correct object/preposition in the picture or by saying a word like "on" or "under" while pointing.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, listening to students' attempts at reading (pointing to words) and using location words. The teacher encourages polite requests by prompting, "Can you show me, please?" if a student points without asking, aligning with sociolinguistic competence.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in identifying words and pictures, collaborative interaction in guiding each other through the story, and communicative task completion by responding to location questions based on the text and illustrations. Emphasis is on reading comprehension through visual-text connection and demonstrating understanding of prepositions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the collaborative reading activity and a quick individual check for word-picture matching at the end.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are individually shown a flashcard with a simple sentence (e.g., "The pen is on the desk.") and a choice of two pictures (one showing the pen on the desk, another showing it under the desk). Students point to the picture that matches the sentence read by the teacher.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are asked to point to one familiar letter (K, L, M, N, or O) within a word presented on a flashcard (e.g., pointing to 'O' in 'book').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Tries to articulate target sounds (/w/, /b/) and words clearly. (Satisfactory / Needs Support)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Accurately points to or identifies words like 'in,' 'on,' 'under,' 'book,' 'desk,' 'pencil' when matched with pictures. (Satisfactory / Needs Support)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity (Reading):</b> Clearly indicates the correct picture when presented with a written sentence. (Satisfactory / Needs Support)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Letter Recognition:</b> Can point to and name at least 2-3 of the target letters (K, L, M, N, O) when asked. (Satisfactory / Needs Support)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I try to read (point to words) in the story?" (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I find the letter 'O'?" (Students point to 'O' on a letter chart or a word card).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite part of reading 'Where Is My Pencil?'?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me where the book is on your desk?" (Practical application and immediate feedback.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? The use of the Big Book and individual picture story booklets seemed to effectively engage students and support their early reading efforts. Choral reading and visual matching were strong for participation. Letter recognition of K, L, M, N, O was partially successful; some students grasped it quickly, others needed more prompting.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson? More explicit modeling of pointing to words one-to-one during choral reading. For struggling students, additional small group support focusing on specific letter sounds and matching words to pictures. Ensure all prepositions are clearly illustrated.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The reading practice in this lesson will directly support Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction). Students will use the location vocabulary (prepositions) and objects from the story to actively ask and answer "Where is it?" questions in communicative tasks, building on their visual comprehension from today's reading activity.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>